{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"James Alexander MacLaren (4 January 1870 – 8 July 1952) was an English cricketer who played first-class cricket for Lancashire County Cricket Club between 1891 and 1894.","title":"James MacLaren (cricketer)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Born in Whalley Range, Manchester, and the eldest of seven brothers, MacLaren was a keen cricketer from his youngest days.","title":"James MacLaren (cricketer)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" He was particularly close to his younger brother Archie, who later captained England.","title":"James MacLaren (cricketer)"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" He attended Elstree School and Harrow School, where he coached as a cricketer and was later joined by Archie.","title":"James MacLaren (cricketer)"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" He also received coaching at Old Trafford Cricket Ground, where his father was treasurer of Lancashire County Cricket Club.","title":"James MacLaren (cricketer)"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" He played in the Harrow first team between 1886 and 1888, and was appointed captain in his final year.","title":"James MacLaren (cricketer)"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" Upon leaving Harrow, he played on four occasions for Lancashire between 1891 and 1894 but had little success.","title":"James MacLaren (cricketer)"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" On one occasion in 1894, he opened the batting with his brother Archie, who was captain of Lancashire by that stage.","title":"James MacLaren (cricketer)"},{"sentence_id":8,"text":" In 1900, he qualified as a doctor of medicine.","title":"James MacLaren (cricketer)"},{"sentence_id":9,"text":" He died at the age of 82 following a fall.","title":"James MacLaren (cricketer)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"William Harold "Bill" Ponsford (19 October 1900 – 6 April 1991) was an Australian cricketer.","title":"Bill Ponsford"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Usually playing as an opening batsman, he formed a successful and long-lived partnership opening the batting for Victoria and Australia